            <ArticleTitle>[The mobile-cecum--a contribution to the independence of this disease and to its surgical treatment].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>6-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A mobile caecum can certainly exist perpetually without symptoms. The operation is indicated after a barium enema and upper gastro-intestinal x-ray examination if the symptoms-especially colicky abdominal pain-have been lasting for a long period. Caecum and ascending colon are fixed at the lateral abdominal wall as a &quot;coecocolofixatio lateralis&quot; (Klose 1909). 93.7% of our 35 patients had no postoperative complaints. The operation is not indicated as prophylaxis for volvulus or invagination.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
